WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.612
Merge combines tables using a key.

00:00:02.612 --> 00:00:04.822
Concat does something different.

00:00:04.822 --> 00:00:12.067
Concat is commonly used when tables have the same or compatible columns and we want to stack them

00:00:12.067 --> 00:00:17.057
together. In this example, we have January orders and February orders.

00:00:17.057 --> 00:00:21.082
Both tables contain order ID, month, and amount.

00:00:21.082 --> 00:00:26.537
Pd.concat stacks the February rows underneath the January rows.

00:00:26.537 --> 00:00:31.017
Ignore_index equals true creates a fresh continuous index.

00:00:31.017 --> 00:00:34.212
Click Run and inspect the combined table.

00:00:34.212 --> 00:00:41.242
This is a common workflow when data arrives as separate monthly files, regional files, or batches.

00:00:41.242 --> 00:00:47.802
Before concatenating many files, check that the columns have compatible meanings and data types.

00:00:47.802 --> 00:00:54.712
For example, one file may call a column amount while another calls it sales_amount.

00:00:54.712 --> 00:01:00.357
One month may store numbers as numeric values while another stores them as text.

00:01:00.357 --> 00:01:05.233
Concat is simple, but the consistency of the source tables still matters.
